    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "48 hours seems like a massive amount of time for just a record change. is there something actually happening in the background during that window or is it just waiting for timers to expire?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right that we might think of it as a wait, but there's active processing going on behind the scenes. When you update a DNS record, that change is propagated to distributed DNS servers, which then replicate and propagate the updated records independently, depending on the TTL, or Time To Live, set on your DNS records. This propagation is not tied to a timer that expires, but rather a natural procedure where each DNS server must update its records independently before others around the world do so with your record. Once all mirrors are updated, the change becomes effective globally, and new users accessing your client's site will see the updated IP address.",
