    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so if its all just caching and TTL, why does it sometimes feel so inconsistent? like sometimes it updates in 10 mins and other times it stays stuck on the old one for hours. feels like some parts of the internet just refuse to acknowledge the change.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "DNS propagation times can indeed be variable because of caching levels at different servers and TTL values set for your DNS records. Some servers might have cache expiry configured to a lower TTL, prompting them to check for updates sooner, while others might have a longer TTL. Additionally, geographic location and intermediary network elements like content delivery networks can also affect how quickly a change is recognized. Sometimes, after the TTL expires and servers are updated, intermediaries might not have refreshed their cache, leading to inconsistent propagation to end-users in different regions. There's no way to know exactly when outdated caches will expire, as it's dependent on your TTL value and the behavior of those networks across the globe.",
      "source": "target",
